% dossier-function: original two-part interlock and layered-form study for Grade 8 on classroom barred instruments (or recorders, keyboards, bells): two groups learn to make one line together without colliding, then shape the form by adding, swapping and thinning layers, alongside listening to a Balinese gamelan composition by I Nyoman Windha
% dossier-elements: eight bars in 4/4; C pentatonic C D E G A plus C5 in Line B; Line A written range C4-A4, Line B D4-C5; quarter notes in bars 1-2 and 7 (the core line, alone), eighth notes alternating with eighth rests in bars 3-6 so the two lines interlock, half notes on shared unisons in bars 4 and 6, and a closing C4 (Line A) and C5 (Line B) on beat 3 of bar 8; composite line in bar 3 C-E-D-G-E-A-G-C5, bar 4 A-C5-G-A then E4 together, bar 5 G-E-A-D-C5-E-A-G, bar 6 G-A-E-G then D4 together; no leap within either line larger than a perfect fourth (A4-E4 in Line B, bar 4; G4-D4 in both lines, bar 6)
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 112 is the target; start at 76 with each group saying its rhythm ("play-rest" for Line A, "rest-play" for Line B) before playing. C is the rehearsal key; both lines' written ranges (C4-A4 and D4-C5) sit inside the young-unison C4-D5 and suit glockenspiels, metallophones, xylophones and recorders. Teach the core line (bars 1-2) to everyone, then Line A's on-beat version, then Line B's off-beat notes against a steady clap, then put them together slowly; swap groups for bars 5-6. The success test is a single even stream of eighths with no gaps and no doubled notes
% dossier-accompaniment: The two written lines are the whole texture, so mute the page's Band in the mixer for the interlock work; a teacher may add one low C on a bass bar or a suspended cymbal at bars 4 and 8 as punctuation. If the Band plays, the folk style (boom-chick guitar on chords found from the melody) is a neutral stand-in; the batch draft's drone style was dropped because the page's drone is a tanpura and tabla, another tradition's sound. No Balinese tuning, instrument name or colotomic gong cycle is imitated
% dossier-lyrics: Instrumental for barred percussion, bells, recorders or keyboards; no words are supplied
% dossier-honesty: Original AI-assisted course composition generated for Grand Central Stationery on 11 November 2026 under human-directed range, texture and teaching constraints. It is not traditional, not collected from a community, not modelled on any Windha passage and not gamelan repertoire; interlocking two parts is a general technique (hocket in European terms), and the reference is a listening bridge only. Drafted as "Gamelan City"; retitled because the piece is explicitly not gamelan
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0. Grand Central Stationery dedicates the two lines, form plan, improvisation cues and this ABC encoding to the public domain to the extent legally possible. Windha's compositions and their recordings remain their owners' copyright and are not included
% dossier-respect: Name I Nyoman Windha as a Balinese composer and teacher, the island of Bali, the chosen ensemble and the piece's dance; explain that gamelan gong kebyar is a twentieth-century Balinese ensemble whose paired instruments are tuned slightly apart to make a shimmer, and that its interlocking parts (kotekan) have their own names and rules. Say plainly that equal-tempered school instruments cannot reproduce gamelan tuning and that this study is not gamelan
% dossier-review: Balinese gamelan educator, Grade 8 music teacher, second score reader and student listening panel; all reviews still required
