N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit (A = Line A, B = Line B): bars 1-2 A alone in quarters C-D-E-G, A-G then E4 half, B rests; bar 3 A on the beats C-D-E-G with eighth rests between, B in the gaps E-G-A-C5; bar 4 A on beats 1-2 A-G, B in the gaps C5-A, then both on E4 for two beats; bar 5 the roles swap, B on the beats G-A-C5-A, A in the gaps E-D-E-G; bar 6 B G-E on the beats, A A-G in the gaps, then both on D4 for two beats; bar 7 A alone in quarters C-D-E-G, B rests (its improvisation window); bar 8 A A-G-E-D eighths and C4 half, B two beats' rest and C5 half, an octave apart on beat 3. Every bar of both parts totals eight eighths, and in bars 3-6 the two lines never sound a new note at the same moment except on the shared unisons. Range A C4-A4, B D4-C5. The batch draft's Line B only repeated Line A's notes an eighth later under held quarters, so the parts doubled rather than interlocked, and bars 4 and 8 were unison whole notes. Originality check: the core line C-D-E-G-A-G-E is a common pentatonic shape and common property; no Windha passage or kotekan pattern is used. The analyzer reads only Line A and so places it in Grade 3 (two parts, pentatonic); the Grade 8 concepts, polyphonic texture and layered form, are in how the two lines combine, which it does not read.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 8 groups can hold the off-beat line at quarter note = 112, whether the composite line sounds as one melody on school instruments, and whether the class prefers the Band muted or in the folk style have not been tested. A specific Windha recording still has to be chosen and verified; a Balinese gamelan educator must review the framing and the new title; a classroom trial, a second score reader and a student listening panel remain required before publication.
